From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 13:10:15 +0200
Subject: net: dsa: lantiq: add the GSWIP 3.0 switch driver for xRX500

The two switch macros of this SoC family run a switch core that is a
superset of the one the common function library already drives, at the same
register offsets, so the parser, VLAN, address table and per-port code apply
unchanged. Add the model on top of that core, with the parts the earlier
models do not have described as per-model data:

 - the macro's top block, which holds the global control register and the
   MDIO master, so the master's registers are named there rather than in a
   region of their own,
 - MAC blocks numbered from port 1 upwards, with no block for port 0 and the
   second macro's external port sharing the first block,
 - RMON counter banks offset from the port numbers above the eighth port,
 - the MDIO master's own enable and the settle delay its busy bit needs.

The register map asks regmap for the little-endian value format rather than
the native one the GSWIP-2.x driver asks for. The two are not the same thing
here: the format regmap calls native pairs with a platform whose
memory-mapped accessors themselves swap on a big-endian build, and this
platform's do not, so the native format would put a byte swap in the path
that nothing undoes and the version register would read 0x30000000 instead
of 0x0030. The comment says so, because the neighbouring driver's choice
looks like the one to copy and is not.

The integrated PHYs are brought up here too. Each core's firmware address
is programmed while it is held in reset, out of an image staged in ordinary
memory and mapped for the device, because the cores fetch outside the
coherence domain the platform sets up. Every core is reset and re-pointed
rather than left alone, because both images answer the identification
registers alike: nothing the driver can read tells a core already staged
with the right image from one that is not. The image is read without the
usermode helper, so a build that probes before the file system is up fails
at once instead of waiting a minute and then bringing up no ethernet at
all; that is also why the driver is a module. MODULE_FIRMWARE names the
in-tree image, so that an image builder assembling a root filesystem from
module dependencies knows the blob has to be there; the string the driver
actually asks for comes from the device tree.

The reset controller writes the request before it confirms it, so a failed
confirmation is not a reason to stop: a line that really did not move shows
up as a PHY that never answers, which the readiness wait reports by name.
The confirmation is only as good as the status bit the device tree names,
and the status register is a remap of the request register rather than a
mirror of it, so a node that repeats its request bit in the second cell
watches an unrelated bit and can only ever time out.

Before the bus is registered every described PHY is given a bounded wait to
answer its status register, because these cores answer their identification
registers first and a status read in that window succeeds and returns zero,
which the PHY layer would freeze as the port's abilities for the rest of the
boot. The wait fails open and says which core did not answer.

The per-PHY array is allocated once rather than on every probe attempt: a
missing conduit defers this probe and it runs again.

The device tree binding for the two compatibles is added separately.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-1
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

